WEBVTT

00:00.580 --> 00:02.260
Let's take the
indefinite integral

00:02.260 --> 00:07.990
of the square root
of 7x plus 9 dx.

00:07.990 --> 00:10.290
So my first question
to you is, is this

00:10.290 --> 00:14.060
going to be a good case
for u-substitution?

00:14.060 --> 00:16.830
Well, when you look here,
maybe the natural thing

00:16.830 --> 00:20.880
to set to be equal
to u is 7x plus 9.

00:20.880 --> 00:24.204
But do I see its derivative
anywhere over here?

00:24.204 --> 00:24.870
Well, let's see.

00:24.870 --> 00:30.120
If we set u to be
equal to 7x plus 9,

00:30.120 --> 00:33.410
what is the derivative of u
with respect to x going to be?

00:33.410 --> 00:35.310
Derivative of u
with respect to x

00:35.310 --> 00:37.080
is just going to be equal to 7.

00:37.080 --> 00:38.470
Derivative of 7x is 7.

00:38.470 --> 00:40.650
Derivative of 9 is 0.

00:40.650 --> 00:44.190
So do we see a 7 lying
around anywhere over here?

00:44.190 --> 00:45.580
Well, we don't.

00:45.580 --> 00:49.340
But what could we do in order
to have a 7 lying around,

00:49.340 --> 00:53.280
but not change the
value of the integral?

00:53.280 --> 00:55.956
Well, the neat thing-- and we've
seen this multiple times-- is

00:55.956 --> 00:57.330
when you're
evaluating integrals,

00:57.330 --> 01:01.240
scalars can go in and outside
of the integral very easily.

01:01.240 --> 01:05.920
Just to remind ourselves, if I
have the integral of let's say

01:05.920 --> 01:11.690
some scalar a times
f of x dx, this

01:11.690 --> 01:17.420
is the same thing as a times
the integral of f of x dx.

01:17.420 --> 01:19.340
The integral of the
scalar times a function

01:19.340 --> 01:22.650
is equal to the scalar times
the integral of the functions.

01:22.650 --> 01:25.280
So let me put this
aside right over here.

01:25.280 --> 01:28.630
So with that in mind, can
we multiply and divide

01:28.630 --> 01:31.710
by something that will
have a 7 showing up?

01:31.710 --> 01:34.330
Well, we can multiply
and divide by 7.

01:34.330 --> 01:35.830
So imagine doing this.

01:35.830 --> 01:38.580
Let's rewrite our
original integral.

01:38.580 --> 01:40.960
So let me draw a
little arrow here just

01:40.960 --> 01:42.420
to go around that aside.

01:42.420 --> 01:44.100
We could rewrite our
original integral

01:44.100 --> 01:51.340
as being 9 to the integral
of times 1/7 times 7 times

01:51.340 --> 01:57.980
the square root of 7x plus 9 dx.

01:57.980 --> 02:00.064
And if we want to, we
could take the 1/7 outside

02:00.064 --> 02:00.730
of the integral.

02:00.730 --> 02:02.270
We don't have to,
but we can rewrite

02:02.270 --> 02:06.620
this as 1/7 times
the integral of 7,

02:06.620 --> 02:12.130
times the square
root of 7x plus 9 dx.

02:12.130 --> 02:14.840
So now if we set u
equal to 7x plus 9,

02:14.840 --> 02:16.860
do we have its
derivative laying around?

02:16.860 --> 02:17.410
Well, sure.

02:17.410 --> 02:20.350
The 7 is right over here.

02:20.350 --> 02:23.000
We know that du-- if we want to
write it in differential form--

02:23.000 --> 02:27.060
du is equal to 7 times dx.

02:27.060 --> 02:31.460
So du is equal to 7 times dx.

02:31.460 --> 02:35.320
That part right over
there is equal to du.

02:35.320 --> 02:37.320
And if we want to care
about u, well, that's

02:37.320 --> 02:40.050
just going to be the 7x plus 9.

02:40.050 --> 02:41.520
That is are u.

02:41.520 --> 02:45.272
So let's rewrite this indefinite
integral in terms of u.

02:45.272 --> 02:53.120
It's going to be equal to
1/7 times the integral of--

02:53.120 --> 02:55.160
and I'll just take the 7
and put it in the back.

02:55.160 --> 02:57.710
So we could just
write the square root

02:57.710 --> 03:06.450
of u du, 7 times dx is du.

03:06.450 --> 03:10.110
And we can rewrite this if we
want as u to the 1/2 power.

03:10.110 --> 03:12.320
It makes it a little bit
easier for us to kind of do

03:12.320 --> 03:14.490
the reverse power rule here.

03:14.490 --> 03:20.940
So we can rewrite this as equal
to 1/7 times the integral of u

03:20.940 --> 03:23.751
to the 1/2 power du.

03:23.751 --> 03:25.000
And let me just make it clear.

03:25.000 --> 03:26.680
This u I could have
written in white

03:26.680 --> 03:27.960
if I want it the same color.

03:27.960 --> 03:31.100
And this du is the same
du right over here.

03:31.100 --> 03:35.790
So what is the antiderivative
of u to the 1/2 power?

03:35.790 --> 03:39.050
Well, we increment
u's power by 1.

03:39.050 --> 03:41.010
So this is going to be
equal to-- let me not

03:41.010 --> 03:43.260
forget this 1/7 out front.

03:43.260 --> 03:49.110
So it's going to be 1/7 times--
if we increment the power here,

03:49.110 --> 03:55.520
it's going to be u to the 3/2,
1/2 plus 1 is 1 and 1/2 or 3/2.

03:55.520 --> 03:57.280
So it's going to
be u to the 3/2.

04:01.030 --> 04:04.140
And then we're going to
multiply this new thing

04:04.140 --> 04:07.980
times the reciprocal
of 3/2, which is 2/3.

04:07.980 --> 04:10.840
And I encourage you to verify
the derivative of 2/3 u

04:10.840 --> 04:14.630
to the 3/2 is
indeed u to the 1/2.

04:14.630 --> 04:15.750
And so we have that.

04:15.750 --> 04:17.450
And since we're
multiplying 1/7 times

04:17.450 --> 04:19.440
this entire indefinite
integral, we

04:19.440 --> 04:21.560
could also throw in a
plus c right over here.

04:21.560 --> 04:23.430
There might have
been a constant.

04:23.430 --> 04:25.990
And if we want, we can
distribute the 1/7.

04:25.990 --> 04:36.170
So it would get 1/7 times
2/3 is 2/21 u to the 3/2.

04:36.170 --> 04:38.750
And 1/7 times some
constant, well, that's

04:38.750 --> 04:40.210
just going to be some constant.

04:40.210 --> 04:41.959
And so I could write
a constant like that.

04:41.959 --> 04:44.510
I could call that c1 and
then I could call this c2,

04:44.510 --> 04:47.140
but it's really just
some arbitrary constant.

04:47.140 --> 04:47.850
And we're done.

04:47.850 --> 04:49.183
Oh, actually, no we aren't done.

04:49.183 --> 04:51.520
We still just have our
entire thing in terms of u.

04:51.520 --> 04:54.290
So now let's unsubstitute it.

04:54.290 --> 05:01.290
So this is going to be equal
to 2/21 times u to the 3/2.

05:01.290 --> 05:03.710
And we already know
what u is equal to.

05:03.710 --> 05:06.060
u is equal to 7x plus 9.

05:06.060 --> 05:08.720
Let me put a new color here
just to ease the monotony.

05:08.720 --> 05:13.460
So it's going to be
2/21 times 7x plus 9

05:13.460 --> 05:18.770
to the 3/2 power plus c.

05:21.870 --> 05:23.030
And we are done.

05:23.030 --> 05:25.500
We were able to take a kind
of hairy looking integral

05:25.500 --> 05:28.120
and realize that even
though it wasn't completely

05:28.120 --> 05:31.869
obvious at first, that
u-substitution is applicable.
